    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlers from Virginia and North Carolina were drawn to LaFayette by its rich clay soils, ideal for growing tobacco and corn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lafayette_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "LaFayette is home to the Lafayette Methodist Church, an antebellum building constructed in 1852 by Dutch builder Daniel Umbenhour.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LaFayette%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Illinois Central Railroad extended its line through LaFayette in 1879, facilitating the transport of tobacco to markets in Louisville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lafayette_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "LaFayette was first settled in the late 1810s and was formally incorporated by the state assembly in 1836.",
